 \"Water meter\"\n  },\n  \"model_family\": {\n    \"family_name\": null,\n    \"models\": [ ... 28 entries ... ]\n  },\n  \"matrix_tables\": [ {...} ],\n  \"characteristics\": {\n    \"model_level\": [ 3 entries ]\n  },\n  \"manufacturers\": [ 4 entries ],\n  \"revision_history\": [ 7 entries ]\n}\n\nWait — is date_issued fabrication? The certificate revision 6 dated 20 May 2022 — the current revision date. This is standard: current certificate issue date = date of current revision. I'll include it. Alternatively omit to be safe. Hmm. The header block \"Number ... revision 6\" followed later by revision table with rev 6 date. I think including date_issued: \"2022-05-20\" is a sound extraction. I'll include.\n\nFor model_family — should I include given it's a fragment? Yes, models are clearly defined by the table.\n\nOne question: should the matrix table be model_level or config_level? The meter size axis... In R137 example, sizes were models. I'll treat DN sizes as models. Fine.\n\nAlso description of modification strings: keep as given, e.g. \"Additional evaluation of reduced bore larger sizes up to DN600\".\n\nDate formats: ISO. \"02 August 2021\" → \"2021-08-02\".\n\nLet me double check revision \"Initial\" — schema example says revision string e.g. \"0\". I'll use \"Initial\" verbatim. Hmm, maybe \"0\" is cleaner... The rule says revision (string). \"Initial\" is a string. I'll keep \"Initial\".\n\nOutput only JSON, no fences.\n\nLet me also double check numbers: 9999999 (7 nines), 99999999 (8 nines), 999999999 (9 nines), 99999 (5), 999999 (6). From table: \nRow1: 9999999 / 99999 / 0,001\nRow2: 9999999 / 999999 / 0,001\nRow3: 9999999 / 999999 / 0,01\nRow4: 9999999 / 9999999 / 0,01\nRow5: 99999999 / 9999999 / 0,1\nRow6: 99999999 / 99999999 / 0,1\nRow7: 999999999 / 99999999 / 1\n\nGood.\n\nNow write the final JSON.",
